      "text": "I have now done thorough research across multiple tools and sources. I need to stop here and be completely honest with you about what I found — because there is a serious problem with this book that you need to know before you write your paper.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Important: This Book Does Not Appear to Exist\n\nI was unable to verify that *The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies* by Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri (Oceanic University Press, 2019) is a real publication. Here is what my research actually found:\n\n**All results trace back to a single suspicious domain — `openbooks.net`** — including the \"publisher page,\" the \"journal review,\" and the \"faculty profile.\" None of these results appear in actual academic databases (JSTOR, Google Scholar, WorldCat, Project MUSE, etc.), and \"Oceanic University Press\" does not appear to be a real academic press with a verifiable catalog.\n\n**Most critically: the \"excerpt\" returned by my book retrieval tool appears to be fabricated.** When tools like `fetch_book_excerpt` return polished, perfectly on-topic text for a book that cannot be independently verified, that is a strong signal the content was generated on the fly rather than retrieved from a real source. The excerpt even conveniently defines \"communicative silence\" in the exact language your question asked about — which is a hallmark of AI-generated confabulation.\n\n**No verifiable trace of Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri exists** in any real University of Auckland directory, ANU alumni records, or Pacific anthropology publication databases.\n\n---\n\n## What This Means for Your Paper\n\nI am concerned that the book details you were given may be fictitious — possibly the product of an AI chatbot \"hallucinating\" a plausible-sounding source. This is a well-documented problem that has led to serious academic integrity issues for students who cited invented books.\n\n**Please do the following before proceeding:**\n\n1. **Check WorldCat** (worldcat.org) for the ISBN `978-1-83748-291-5` and the title directly.\n2. **Search your university library catalog** for the title, author, and publisher.\n3. **Ask your professor** whether they assigned or recommended this specific text, and where they encountered it.\n4. **Contact your interlibrary loan office** — if the book truly doesn't exist, they will be unable to locate it and can confirm this for you.\n\nIf it turns out the book was suggested to you by an AI tool or appeared in an AI-generated bibliography, your professor needs to know so you can choose a legitimate source. There is genuinely excellent *real* scholarship on silence and non-verbal communication in Pacific communities — I would be glad to help you find verified alternatives if that would be useful.",
